    "result_text": "Green. Both findings were valid and I addressed both.\n\n## Changes\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 Duplicated SHA-256\u2192hex boilerplate (agreed, fixed)**\nExtracted a new package-private `Sha256` helper with a single `hex(String)` method (UTF-8 digest \u2192 lower-case hex). Both call sites now delegate to it, choosing only the case/length they need:\n- `OwnerIdentity.key(...)` \u2192 `Sha256.hex(raw)` (full, lower-case).\n- `OwnerHousehold.idFor(...)` \u2192 `Sha256.hex(key).substring(0, 12).toUpperCase()` (first 12, upper-case).\n\nBoth classes dropped their `MessageDigest`/`NoSuchAlgorithmException` imports and hand-rolled loops. The household id is byte-for-byte identical (upper-casing the first 12 lower-hex chars equals the former `%02X` output), so behaviour is unchanged.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 Stale javadoc still referencing \"household\" in the key (agreed, fixed)**\nUpdated all three comments to describe the new composition (telephone / email / last name's Soundex):\n- `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique.java:11` \u2014 resolving the self-contradiction with its own second paragraph.\n- `DuplicateIdentityException.java:5`\n- `DuplicateIdentityExceptionHandler.java:11`\n\n(`OwnerHousehold.idFor`'s own javadoc still accurately says \"first 12 hex characters, upper-cased\", so it was left as-is.)\n\nFull test suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 2/2 acceptance tests green.",
